[THOUGHT] Confirmation of combat exposure satisfies DSM-5 Criterion A for PTSD. Symptoms (intrusive dreams, flashbacks, anxiety) beginning 7 weeks ago—i.e., >1 month post-trauma—meet the duration criterion for PTSD (not ASD). Next, we must assess whether the patient meets ≥1 symptom from each of the four PTSD clusters:  
- Intrusion (already endorsed: dreams, flashbacks);  
- Avoidance (e.g., avoiding thoughts, places, people linked to trauma);  
- Negative alterations in cognition/mood (e.g., persistent negative beliefs, diminished interest, estrangement, inability to experience positive emotions);  
- Alterations in arousal and reactivity (e.g., hypervigilance, exaggerated startle, irritability, sleep disturbance).  

Of these, avoidance is highly specific and diagnostically necessary—and its presence strongly supports PTSD over other anxiety or mood disorders. So the single most discriminating next question is whether the patient actively avoids trauma-related stimuli.

[QUERY] In the past month, have you been deliberately avoiding thoughts, feelings, conversations, activities, places, or people that remind you of your combat experience?
